{
  "dom_id": "question-166363",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "From the given data, the best backward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \ncustomers per month.",
  "worked_solution": "Again, we use the fact that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1)-q(1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{59-0}{1} \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a backward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $59$ customers per month.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
